{"answer":"The monitors will be fishermen who have volunteered for the community’s stewardship program. They are elected each quarter by a simple majority vote of all fishing households, and their election is recorded on the communal ledger. Once elected, they receive a written mandate from the communal charter that grants them authority to conduct the quarterly sonar surveys, calculate the lake’s stock, publish the 10% limits, and enforce the 25% reserve rule. The charter also empowers them to impose the temporary moratorium and levy fines when the lake’s stock falls below the 25% threshold. The fines are paid directly to the community’s reserve fund account. The monitors keep a log of any violations and present it to the rotating committee for adjudication, but they can issue the fine immediately when a clear rule breach is observed. All their actions are documented on the communal ledger so everyone can see their work and the fines collected.

This does not alter any of the rules in the original proposal; it simply adds the detail of who the monitors are and how they are chosen and authorized.

